@schema agentdocs/v1
@id agentdoc-dsl-specification
@title "AgentDoc DSL Specification"
@kind specification
@description
AgentDoc is a versioned directive DSL for standardized human-facing vault documents whose HTML, CSS, navigation, and repeated summaries are generated deterministically. It reduces authoring instructions and token use while preserving Markdown, trusted HTML fragments, visual decision options, and a deliberately conspicuous CSS escape hatch.
@end-description

@references
@reference doc=warlander/unified-documentation-standard label="Unified Documentation Standard"
@reference doc=agentdocs/multi-format-document-architecture label="Multi-Format Document Architecture"
@end-references

@evaluations
@evaluation metric=size level=2
The change spans parsing, rendering, identity, latest and historical reads, search, navigation, CLI behavior, tests, and shared instructions.
@end-evaluation
@evaluation metric=complexity level=2
Identity, migrations, canonical rendering, and Git-backed history must agree across every read and write path.
@end-evaluation
@evaluation metric=risk level=2
Incorrect identity or migration behavior could fork documents or hide history while existing HTML and Markdown must remain unchanged.
@end-evaluation
@end-evaluations

@decisions
@decision id=D1 status=locked by=human
@question
Who owns standard document structure and presentation?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Structured source and renderer-owned presentation"
Agents author directives, attributes, Markdown, and rare HTML/CSS fragments; the renderer owns the shell, theme, tables, badges, responsive layouts, and navigation hooks.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D2 status=locked by=human layout=full
@question
What is the canonical rendered order?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Fixed semantic order"
Title → kind → description → references → evaluations → decision log → open decisions → locked decisions → risks → tests → implementation steps → freeform sections, with absent optional sections omitted.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D3 status=locked by=human
@question
Which initial document kinds are supported?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Five extensible kinds"
The registry starts with `specification`, `implementation-plan`, `exploration`, `handoff`, and `design-doc`; later kinds can be added without changing authored documents.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D4 status=locked by=human
@question
How are decision state and ownership represented?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Separate state and final decider"
Decision state is `open`, `locked`, or `removed`, while locked decisions record `human` or `ai` as the final definite decider.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D5 status=locked by=human
@question
Where is rich content permitted?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Explicit Markdown, HTML, and CSS escape hatches"
Options and freeform sections choose Markdown or trusted HTML explicitly, while document CSS is permitted only when generated styles cannot express a necessary design.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D6 status=locked by=human
@question
How is source compatibility versioned?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Schema versions independent from Git revisions"
Every source declares a schema version, and older supported schemas migrate sequentially into the current typed model without confusing format changes with human document revisions.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D7 status=locked by=human
@question
Which syntax keeps long Markdown and HTML blocks natural?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Line directives with verbatim bodies"
Column-one directives avoid indentation and quoting overhead, while named end directives preserve long rich-content blocks nearly verbatim.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D8 status=locked by=human
@question
How is document identity kept stable across title edits?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Required immutable source ID"
A required lowercase slug-like ID owns the vault path, allowing the human-readable title to change without forking history.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D9 status=locked by=human
@question
Which estimation model is used?
@end-question
@selected id=B format=markdown title="Size, Complexity, and Risk with reasons"
Each applicable document records all three 0–3 ratings, and a required one-sentence rationale makes every score interpretable instead of decorative.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D10 status=locked by=human
@question
When are old schemas upgraded?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Non-mutating migration on latest reads"
Latest reads migrate and render stored source in memory, while historical reads retain their committed HTML snapshot and no implicit read creates a Git revision.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D11 status=locked by=human
@question
Should implementation steps remain structured?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Stable steps with verification"
Implementation plans require ordered step IDs, actions, and verification checks so execution order remains intentional and testable.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D12 status=locked by=ai
@question
Which name distinguishes semantic document kind from source format?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Use kind for semantics"
`kind` avoids collision with the renderer registry's existing `type` field.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D13 status=locked by=ai
@question
When may invalid input mutate the vault?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Only after complete validation"
Parsing, migration, validation, and rendering must all succeed before filesystem, database, search, or Git writes begin.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D14 status=locked by=ai
@question
How do internal references synchronize the vault UI?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Fallback links plus validated host messages"
Rendered references remain usable URLs and also send a validated navigation event so iframe content, selected row, header, search, and version controls move together.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D15 status=locked by=human
@question
Which source order is semantically meaningful?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Only intentionally ordered siblings"
Top-level blocks and named child fields are position-independent; only options, implementation steps, and freeform sections preserve authored sibling order.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D16 status=locked by=human
@question
When should agents reread generated documents in a headless browser?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Only for a stated visual or runtime concern"
Normal verification uses parser, renderer, and structural tests; browser rereads require a concrete layout, interaction, or rendering concern that deterministic checks cannot answer.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D17 status=locked by=human layout=full
@question
Which standard appearance should generated tables use?
@end-question
@selected id=F format=html title="Tinted index column with alternating rows"
<div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Risk</th><th>Severity</th><th>Test</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>R1</td><td>Duplicate document history</td><td>2</td><td>T4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>R4</td><td>Stale navigation selection</td><td>2</td><td>T5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>R8</td><td>Reduced visual readability</td><td>1</td><td>T12</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p>The table uses a contained header and border, a continuous tinted identity column, and subtly alternating row backgrounds outside that column.</p>
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D18 status=locked by=human layout=full
@question
Which standard appearance should evaluations use?
@end-question
@selected id=E format=html title="Reasoned capsule rows"
<div class="evaluation-panel">
  <article class="evaluation-row tone-warn">
    <strong class="evaluation-metric">SIZE</strong>
    <div class="evaluation-scale" aria-label="size 2 of 3"><span>0</span><span>1</span><span class="active">2</span><span>3</span></div>
    <p class="evaluation-reason">The change crosses several systems.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="evaluation-row tone-ok">
    <strong class="evaluation-metric">COMPLEXITY</strong>
    <div class="evaluation-scale" aria-label="complexity 1 of 3"><span>0</span><span class="active">1</span><span>2</span><span>3</span></div>
    <p class="evaluation-reason">The implementation path is clear.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="evaluation-row tone-bad">
    <strong class="evaluation-metric">RISK</strong>
    <div class="evaluation-scale" aria-label="risk 3 of 3"><span>0</span><span>1</span><span>2</span><span class="active">3</span></div>
    <p class="evaluation-reason">The affected history behavior needs careful verification.</p>
  </article>
</div>
<p>Each metric keeps its semantic green, yellow, or red color; the active segment in the connected 0–3 capsule is the sole score, and the rationale sits directly beneath the scale.</p>
@end-selected
@end-decision
@decision id=D19 status=locked by=human
@question
How should expanded locked decisions use horizontal space?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Three-column grid with local full-width override"
Locked decisions tile up to three per row by default, collapse responsively, and accept `layout=full` on an individual decision when its selected content needs extra horizontal space.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D20 status=locked by=human
@question
Where should acceptance criteria be represented?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Testing is the acceptance contract"
Acceptance criteria become explicit test rows with a tester, method, and status instead of a separate freeform section.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@decision id=D21 status=locked by=human
@question
How should a locked decision card distinguish its semantic parts?
@end-question
@selected id=A format=markdown title="Labeled problem, decision, and description hierarchy"
Each card labels and separates the original problem, places the winning decision in a tinted inset panel, and presents its supporting description below a divider.
@end-selected
@end-decision

@end-decisions

@risks
@risk id=R1 kind=edge-case severity=2 tests=T4
@description
A title rename could create a duplicate history instead of updating one stable document.
@end-description
@end-risk
@risk id=R2 kind=edge-case severity=2 tests=T2
@description
An unknown or malformed schema could be guessed or partially persisted.
@end-description
@end-risk
@risk id=R3 kind=edge-case severity=2 tests=T1,T3
@description
Duplicate IDs, broken test links, or invalid decision-state combinations could produce misleading summaries.
@end-description
@end-risk
@risk id=R4 kind=edge-case severity=2 tests=T5
@description
An internal link could navigate only the iframe and leave the previous sidebar row selected.
@end-description
@end-risk
@risk id=R5 kind=general severity=2 tests=T6
@description
Rich HTML or malformed CSS could execute behavior or escape the renderer-owned style scope.
@end-description
@end-risk
@risk id=R6 kind=general severity=1 tests=T8,T9
@description
Source-aware latest rendering could change historical snapshots or regress existing HTML and Markdown documents.
@end-description
@end-risk
@risk id=R7 kind=edge-case severity=1 tests=T11
@description
Moving metadata or named child blocks could accidentally change semantics despite the position-independent contract.
@end-description
@end-risk
@risk id=R8 kind=general severity=1 tests=T12
@description
A prettier table or evaluation treatment could reduce density, contrast, or narrow-screen readability.
@end-description
@end-risk
@end-risks

@tests
@test id=T1 tester=unit status=success
@description
Parser fields, vocabularies, IDs, cross-references, escaping, and aggregated diagnostics.
@end-description
@method
Run table-driven valid and invalid AgentDoc fixtures.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T2 tester=unit status=success
@description
Current-schema parsing, unknown-version rejection, and non-mutating latest rendering.
@end-description
@method
Open v1 and unsupported fixtures and compare the source and committed HTML before and after reads.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T3 tester=unit status=success
@description
Open, locked, and removed decision invariants and canonical rendering.
@end-description
@method
Assert log order, option layouts, AI opinion, selected winner, and removed outcomes in rendered output.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T4 tester=unit status=success
@description
Canonical title propagation and stable identity.
@end-description
@method
Upload two titles under one ID and assert one path, history, metadata record, HTML title, and search result.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T5 tester=ai status=success
@description
Internal reference event generation, validation, destination lookup, and host selection reset.
@end-description
@method
Assert the renderer bridge and message validator, then inspect the host handler's destination lookup and selected-state reset.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T6 tester=unit status=success
@description
HTML and CSS escape-hatch boundaries.
@end-description
@method
Accept trusted visual fragments and reject scripts, styles, event handlers, JavaScript URLs, and unbalanced CSS scope escapes.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T7 tester=ai status=success
@description
Canonical section order and responsive option layouts.
@end-description
@method
Inspect deterministic heading order and generated grid, full-width, and narrow-screen CSS rules.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T8 tester=unit status=success
@description
Latest source rendering versus SHA-pinned historical HTML.
@end-description
@method
Change source without rewriting the committed artifact and assert latest and historical responses diverge correctly.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T9 tester=unit status=success
@description
HTML and Markdown regression safety across upload, render, history, diff, reindex, and document-origin paths.
@end-description
@method
Run the complete workspace test suite, typecheck, and production build.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T10 tester=ai status=success
@description
Shared authoring instructions omit generated presentation boilerplate and routine browser rereads.
@end-description
@method
Validate the replacement AgentDoc skill and synchronize the source-managed configuration.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T11 tester=unit status=success
@description
Position-independent source semantics.
@end-description
@method
Permute top-level directives and unordered named children, then assert an identical normalized model.
@end-method
@end-test
@test id=T12 tester=unit status=success
@description
The selected table, evaluation, and locked-decision treatments preserve identity, status color, semantic hierarchy, rationale proximity, and narrow-screen behavior.
@end-description
@method
Assert contained tables with tinted first columns and alternating rows, colored 0–3 evaluation capsules with no duplicate score, and labeled problem, decision, and description card regions.
@end-method
@end-test
@end-tests

@section format=markdown title="Directive Grammar"
- A directive begins at column one with `@`; directive and attribute names use lowercase kebab-case.
- `@schema`, `@id`, `@title`, and `@kind` take one scalar; values containing spaces are quoted, with only `\"` and `\\` escapes.
- Complex directives use unique named attributes whose order has no meaning.
- Content blocks close with their matching named `@end-*` directive; bare text outside a content block is invalid.
- Markdown, HTML, and CSS bodies are verbatim until their matching terminator; `@@` at column one emits one literal leading `@`.
- The parser resolves names and scopes into a typed model before rendering; top-level placement never controls output order.
@end-section

@section format=markdown title="Normative Content Rules"
- Title, kind, schema, immutable ID, and a one-paragraph description are required; two to three description sentences are a lint rule.
- Specifications and implementation plans require Size, Complexity, and Risk evaluations; other kinds may omit the trio as a whole.
- Open decisions require at least two ordered options, a valid AI leaning, and an AI comment; `grid` shows up to three cards and `full` uses one row each.
- Locked decisions retain one selected option and final decider, visually separate the problem, winning decision, and description, tile three per row by default, and may use `layout=full`; removed decisions retain only their question and reason in the log.
- Risks use stable `R` IDs, `general|edge-case`, severity 0–3, a description, and one or more valid `T` IDs.
- Acceptance criteria are test rows with explicit methods and statuses, never a separate freeform section.
- Standard tables use a contained header and border, a tinted identity column, and alternating body rows.
- Evaluations use colored reasoned rows with a connected 0–3 capsule whose active segment is the sole score, with the rationale directly beneath its metric scale.
- Freeform sections preserve source order and choose exactly one Markdown or HTML body; scripts and style behavior are invalid.
- Unknown directives, attributes, values, duplicates, broken references, mismatched terminators, and unsafe fragments are hard errors reported before mutation.
@end-section

@section format=markdown title="Integration and Compatibility"
- `.agentdoc` is a third renderer format; existing HTML and Markdown storage and rendering remain unchanged.
- AgentDoc `id` and `title` override filename and upload-title metadata; IDs are globally unique under the vault's existing slug API and collisions return 409.
- Uploads store source plus a committed HTML snapshot only after successful validation.
- Latest reads and reindexing parse and render current source in memory; SHA-pinned reads serve the committed HTML snapshot.
- The migration registry advances supported schemas sequentially without rewriting source during reads; the initial release has no legacy schema migration.
- Internal references use `project/id`, a fallback URL, and a validated iframe-to-host message.
- The shared agent configuration uses the smaller AgentDoc authoring skill and treats HTML as a legacy or explicitly requested raw format.
@end-section
